`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:["NVlabs/flip implementation declaring v1.7 at commit ",e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14"}),". This pins the inspected source, not a resolved release tag or installed package. Scope below is the Python ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"flip_evaluator.evaluate(reference, test, dynamicRangeString, inputsRGB=True, applyMagma=True, computeMeanError=True, parameters={})"})," wrapper and its nanobind/C++ path. The separate PyTorch loss has a different argument order: test first, reference second. Do not transplant its calling convention. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/pytorch/README.md",children:"PyTorch contract"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(n.h4,{id:"contracts-that-can-reverse-candidate-ordering",children:"Contracts that can reverse candidate ordering"}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(n.li,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"Color is an input contract, not inferred from file contents."})," The Python wrapper defaults LDR to encoded sRGB, subsequently decoded by the binding; ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"inputsRGB=False"})," is needed for already-linear LDR arrays. HDR forcibly sets ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"inputsRGB=False"}),", expecting nonnegative linear-sRGB RGB. Passing a display screenshot as HDR, double-decoding linear RGB, feeding ACEScg without a primaries conversion, or treating LPIPS's [-1,1] normalization as reusable changes the question being measured. The LDR C++ path clamps to [0,1]; validate ranges before that silent information loss. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/flip_evaluator/flip_python_api.py",children:"Wrapper, lines 36–100"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/nanobindFLIP.cpp",children:"binding, lines 218–228"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/cpp/FLIP.h",children:"core, lines 2399–2403"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.li,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"Reference identity and exposure policy matter."})," Unspecified HDR start/stop exposures are calculated from the reference's maximum/median luminance. The default count is max(2, ceil(stop-start)); both images receive each shared exposure factor 2^c, the selected tone mapper, and a clamp before LDR-FLIP. The final map retains the ",e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"maximum error at each pixel over exposures"}),", not their average. Swapping reference/test can therefore change automatic exposures; changing candidate-dependent references, exposure ranges/counts, or tone mappers invalidates a shared ranking. Store the resolved parameters. The richer C++ interface exposes the winning-exposure map and intermediate images; the Python tuple does not. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/cpp/FLIP.h",children:"Core, lines 2186–2234, 2314–2404"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.li,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"Tone-mapper spelling is a pinned API trap."})," The wrapper validates ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"parameters['tonemapper'].lower()"})," but passes the original string through. The inspected C++ implementation compares exact lowercase ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"aces"}),", ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"hable"}),", ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"reinhard"}),", with ACES as fallback. Thus the documented title-case ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"Hable"}),"/",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"Reinhard"})," can select ACES in this Python path. Canonicalize and persist lowercase values. This is a static code-path finding, not an executed reproduction; the CLI separately lowercases its input. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/flip_evaluator/flip_python_api.py",children:"Wrapper, lines 82–100"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/nanobindFLIP.cpp",children:"binding, lines 148–151"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/cpp/FLIP.h",children:"core, lines 1395–1435 and 2186–2199"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/cpp/tool/FLIPToolHelpers.h",children:"CLI, lines 122–132"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.li,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"PPD is part of the measurement."})," The core computes distance_m × display_width_px / display_width_m × pi/180; defaults are 0.7 m, 3840 px, 0.7 m (about 67 PPD). Supply either ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"ppd"})," or ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"vc"}),", never both, and preserve image scaling/viewing assumptions. Resizing a capture and retaining the old physical interpretation is not a neutral optimization. Cropping before evaluation also changes spatial-filter context and, for automatic HDR, the reference statistics; pooling a region from a full-frame map answers a different question. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/cpp/FLIP.h",children:"Core, lines 110–123"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/flip_evaluator/flip_python_api.py",children:"wrapper, lines 48–80"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.li,{children:[e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"Raw map, preview, and aggregate are distinct artifacts."})," The Python return is ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"(map, mean, resolved_parameters)"}),". Default ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"applyMagma=True"})," yields H×W×3 display colors, not three independent error channels; use ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"False"})," for H×W×1 numerical errors. The returned mean is the arithmetic mean of the scalar map before colorization. With ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"computeMeanError=False"}),", the Python path returns ",e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"-1"}),", which must never win a lower-is-better selector. Persist the raw map separately from its preview and retain region/tail evidence for small critical defects. NVIDIA's publication page now recommends the mean rather than the original paper's weighted median when one number is needed. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/nanobindFLIP.cpp",children:"Binding, lines 185–238"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/cpp/FLIP.h",children:"core, lines 2444–2489"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://research.nvidia.com/publication/flip",children:"author hindsight"})]}),`
`]}),`
`,e.jsx(n.h4,{id:"harness-side-validity-gate-before-evaluation",children:"Harness-side validity gate, before evaluation"}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:["Canonicalize equal, nonempty H×W×3 RGB arrays with explicit float32/contiguous CPU storage, finite samples, defined range, color transform, crop/registration and alpha treatment. The nanobind signature is a three-dimensional float/CPU/contiguous array; its explicit checks cover dimensionality and H/W agreement, ",e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"not shape(2)==3"}),", before copying three floats per pixel. This missing check is a source observation, not a reproduced memory-safety failure. File input is different: the LDR loader uses three-channel 8-bit ",e.jsx(n.code,{children:"stbi_load"})," divided by 255, and EXR uses plain channel-name recognition; these routes do not preserve an RGBA validation contract or perform a documented alpha composite. Composite deterministically upstream and validate alpha separately. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/nanobindFLIP.cpp",children:"Binding, lines 61–100 and 185–216"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/cpp/tool/imagehelpers.h",children:"loader, lines 65–88 and 133–217"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:["Validate recognized keys, types, finite positive PPD/viewing values, lowercase tone mapper, finite ordered HDR exposure bounds, and an integer exposure count >=2 under a resource budget. In this Python path unknown parameter keys print a message rather than fail. With valid finite explicit exposure bounds, the simplified API initializes a zero map and a supplied zero count skips the HDR loop; a count of one reaches division by count-minus-one. These are static paths that justify rejection, not measured outputs or claims about every CLI/error path. v1.7 clamps a zero median to epsilon, but maximum luminance still divides into the automatic start-exposure calculation: an all-black reference needs an explicit exposure policy or an invalid-result outcome, rather than assuming the median fix covers it. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/nanobindFLIP.cpp",children:"Binding, lines 115–161"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/src/cpp/FLIP.h",children:"core, lines 2210–2233, 2357–2397, 2448–2454"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:["Separate invalid-input/configuration, process/API failure, missing/nonfinite output, and valid measured discrepancy. Some checks exit the process rather than raise a recoverable exception. Require finite [0,1] raw output of the expected dimensions and a valid mean; compare exact metric build/backend/platform because upstream documents small floating-point differences. Proposed unexecuted measurement fixtures: identity pair; one-pixel/UI defect; dark/highlight defect; alpha-only change after explicit compositing; swapped reference; full-frame versus cropped HDR; and all-black reference under a declared valid exposure policy. Separately test wrapper canonicalization of tone-mapper names and ",e.jsx(n.strong,{children:"preflight rejection"})," of malformed/empty/channel-mismatched arrays, NaN/Inf and zero/one exposure counts; do not pass these rejected cases into the unchecked native path. No acceptance threshold is asserted. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/misc/precision.md",children:"Precision note"})]}),`
`,e.jsx(n.h4,{id:"paper-evidence-and-rights-limits",children:"Paper evidence and rights limits"}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:["The 2020 LDR paper's study assessed correspondence of error maps to differences seen while flipping image pairs, using a 0.5 s period, 21 pairs and 42 participants, at instructed 67 PPD. It reports favorable average correspondence; participants were mainly experts, without controlled display calibration. That is not a validation of automated game-candidate selection, a universal threshold, or temporal quality. Its stated limitations include masking, which can cause overestimation. The original paper addresses LDR; the 2021 HDR publication describes a composite over exposure-compensated, tone-mapped pairs. The HDR full PDF exceeded retrieval size limits, so detailed HDR mechanics above are attributed to pinned code, not an unread paper. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://developer-blogs.nvidia.com/wp-content/uploads/2020/07/flip-author-version-reduced-file-size.pdf",children:"LDR paper, §§2 and 6.2"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://research.nvidia.com/publication/2021-05_visualizing-errors-rendered-high-dynamic-range-images",children:"HDR publication"})]}),`
`,e.jsxs(n.p,{children:["Inspected repository code is BSD-3-Clause; bundled TinyEXR and stb have separate notices in the third-party license file. This does not grant blanket rights to papers, research-study images, engine captures, or game assets. Link rather than redistribute the paper/image corpus; use independently cleared fixture images. No learned weights or dataset acquisition are needed for this proposed source-based contract. ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/LICENSE",children:"Code license"}),"; ",e.jsx(n.a,{href:"https://github.com/NVlabs/flip/blob/b475eb4bf394ab877c42166c9eb0a84a02cc5b14/misc/LICENSE-third-party.md",children:"third-party notices"})]}),`
